One common way to introduce vulnerabilities in Java web applications is through **Unsafe Deserialization**. This occurs when an application accepts untrusted data from a user and attempts to deserialize it using the standard Java serialization mechanism, allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.URL;

// This class is a demonstration of a vulnerable deserialization endpoint
public class VulnerableServer {
    public void processRequest(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        // This line is vulnerable because it allows an attacker to 
        // supply a malicious serialized object that triggers 
        // Remote Code Execution (RCE) during the readObject call.
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
        System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.getClass().getName());
    }
}
```
